Acoustic Connect for Capacitor

Acoustic Connect plugin for Capacitor. Requires Capacitor 8 and Node 22 or newer.

New Features

- Analytics API: `enable`, `disable`, `setLogLevel`, `logIdentity`,
  `getSdkVersion`, `isSdkEnabled`, `flushQueues`, `setCurrentScreenName`,
  `logCustomEvent`, `logClickEvent`, `logTextChangeEvent`, `logSignal`,
  `logExceptionEvent`, `logScreenViewContextLoad`, `logScreenViewContextUnload`,
  `logScreenLayout`, `logLocation`, `logLocationUpdate`, `setConfigItem` and
  `getConfigItem`, with TypeScript types. Text passed to `logTextChangeEvent` is
  masked by default
- Dialog events: `logDialogShowEvent`, `logDialogDismissEvent`,
  `logDialogButtonClickEvent` and `logDialogCustomEvent`. They are sent as custom
  events (`DialogShowEvent`, `DialogDismissEvent`, `DialogButtonClickEvent`,
  `DialogCustomEvent`), the same event names and keys as the Cordova plugin
- Configuration in the `AcousticConnect` block of `capacitor.config`: with
  `appKey` and `postMessageUrl` set, the SDK starts when the app launches, so
  the first screen is captured (`autoEnable: false` starts it only from
  `enable()`)
- Config options: `useRelease`, `androidVersion`/`iosVersion` to pin a Connect
  SDK version, `killSwitchEnabled`/`killSwitchUrl`, `locationLoggingEnabled`,
  `screenCaptureEnabled`, and `layoutConfig`, `layoutConfigIos` and
  `layoutConfigAndroid` for screen-capture rules
- Opt-in push (`pushEnabled`, off by default): Android uses the Connect FCM push
  artifact and Firebase Messaging, iOS runs the SDK with push. Without it
  Android builds with the analytics-only artifact and needs no Firebase
- `AcousticConnect.push` API: `requestPermission`, `getPermissionState`, and, for
  iOS manual push mode (`iosPushMode: 'manual'`), `didRegisterWithToken`,
  `didFailToRegister`, `didReceiveNotification` and `didReceiveResponse`
- iOS rich push: `npx acoustic-connect setup-ios-push` adds the Notification
  Service and Content extensions to the Xcode project of a Swift Package Manager
  app. The extensions take the host app's version, as the App Store requires
- `npx acoustic-connect doctor` checks the app's config, Android and iOS
  projects and reports what would go wrong at build time or on the device
- iOS links the Connect SDK through Swift Package Manager or CocoaPods
- On iOS a `logSignal` payload is at the root of `signal` (Connect iOS SDK 2.1.26 and
  newer), as on Android
- `npx acoustic-connect setup-ios-push` adds the rich-push extensions to Swift
  Package Manager and CocoaPods apps (with CocoaPods it adds the pod to the
  Podfile: run `pod install` after it)
- Sample apps: DemoCapacitor and an Angular + Ionic app, with end-to-end checks
  of what the plugin sends

Known Issues

- A notification arriving and being tapped has not been checked with a real push
  on a real device. Registration is checked on the Android emulator and the iOS
  simulator
- Rich push has not been received on a real device (the iOS simulator does not
  show rich media)
- The manual push calls (`didRegisterWithToken` and the others) are checked with
  a fake SDK, not against a real push
- `iosVersion` applies only to apps that use CocoaPods; with Swift Package
  Manager the plugin's `Package.swift` takes the newest 2.x from 2.1.51
- Native masking rules do not reach the page: the SDK sees the WebView as one
  control. Use `screenCaptureEnabled: false` to keep page content out of
  captures
- With Swift Package Manager the app needs a Run Script build phase that copies
  `ConnectResources.bundle` into the app root; without it the SDK sends only part
  of its data (see the README)

Our environment for this release:

- Xcode 26.5
- MacOS 26.5
- Capacitor 8, Node 22
- iOS 15.1 or newer (Connect iOS SDK 2.1.51 or newer, floating)
- Android minSdk 26, SDK 36 (Connect Android SDK 11.1.17)
